    {
      "id": "far-cf-start-and-revenue-c1",
      "heading": "Transaction 1: shares issued for cash",
      "explanation_md": "After incorporating, Circle Films **issues 3,000 shares of common stock to investors for ₹15,000 cash.**\n\n- Cash is received, so **assets rise by ₹15,000**.\n- Investors have contributed cash for an ownership interest, so **equity rises**. More specifically, **common stock rises by ₹15,000**.\n\nTable: Cash goes from ₹0 to ₹15,000 and Common Stock from ₹0 to ₹15,000. Assets ₹15,000 = Liabilities ₹0 + Equity ₹15,000.",
      "example_ids": [
        "far-ex-t1-stock"
      ],
      "key_terms": [
        {
          "term": "Common stock",
          "meaning": "The equity account that rises when investors contribute cash for an ownership interest.",
          "source_ref": "far--practice-circle-films-transaction-analysis#1"
        }
      ],
      "exam_line": "Issuing shares for cash: assets (cash) and equity (common stock) both rise by the amount received.",
      "clarification": null,
      "check_questions": [
        {
