      "retired": false
    },
    {
      "id": "far-cf-start-and-revenue-q-b748ad05",
      "type": "long",
      "stem": "Explain the three types of effect seen in the first three transactions: an increase in assets and equity from owners, an exchange of assets, and revenue. Use Circle Films' numbers.",
      "options": [],
      "answer": "",
      "accept": [],
      "explanation": "",
      "model_answer_md": "**1. Owners contribute (transaction 1).** Investors give ₹15,000 for 3,000 shares. Cash and common stock both rise by ₹15,000.\n\n**2. Exchange of assets (transaction 2).** Cash of ₹10,000 buys a ₹9,000 camera and ₹1,000 of memory cards. Assets both increase and decrease by ₹10,000, so the net effect is no change in total assets.\n\n**3. Revenue (transaction 3).** ₹1,500 is received for filming a wedding. Cash rises, and because the inflow comes from providing a service it is a revenue. Revenues increase net income and retained earnings, so equity rises by ₹1,500.\n\nIn each case assets equal liabilities plus equity afterwards.",
      "rubric": [
        {
          "point": "Owner contribution raises cash and common stock",
          "source_ref": "far--practice-circle-films-transaction-analysis#1"
        },
        {
          "point": "Exchange: no change in total assets",
          "source_ref": "far--practice-circle-films-transaction-analysis#1"
        },
        {
          "point": "Revenue raises retained earnings",
          "source_ref": "far--practice-circle-films-transaction-analysis#1"
        },
